<h1>Byte Decoding Equivalence Article</h1>
<p>This plain English article exists to prove that extracting from raw bytes and from an already decoded HTML string yields exactly the same article data structure.</p>
<p>The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog while a careful engineer watches every byte travel through the decoder pipeline without any silent reinterpretation.</p>
<p>Relative links, metadata, publication hints and readability heuristics must all behave identically regardless of which public entry point the caller chooses.</p>
<p>Padding text follows so the content comfortably exceeds the minimum length threshold enforced by the extraction pipeline in every supported configuration.</p>
<p>A final paragraph closes the fixture with a reproducible sentence used by the equivalence test suite across both supported extraction entry points.</p>

</article>
